    function: "witness_p1_proof_kernel_acceptance_contract_holds" as NonEmptyStr,
  )
}

data roadmap_with_execution_note: String = "The per-node execution binding, shaped as a COMBINATOR rather than a parameter on every constructor (the `sign` twin below is the precedent, and it already carries `execution: rn.execution` through untouched). Threading an execution argument through active/active_small/active_large/authored/derivable/ticket_row would edit seven constructors and every one of their ~46 call sites to express a fact that today holds for exactly one node — the churn a combinator avoids, and the reason `sign` is a combinator too. A node without this wrapper keeps ExecutionContractUnspecified, which is the fail-closed refusal roadmap_execution_contract_frontier_note declares, not a default anyone chose."

data v1_lane_binding_survey_note: String = "A BINDING NAMES THE VALIDATION THAT CLOSES A NODE, so it can only be authored once that validation exists. A node without one stays ExecutionContractUnspecified, which is the honest fail-closed state rather than a gap: dispatch refuses with a typed diagnostic instead of admitting an environment for work whose completion nothing can check. Fabricating contracts to make a lane look dispatchable produces exactly the coverage-by-illusion tier — every node clickable, every Verify vacuous. The closing validation is written as part of the node's first slice; the contract follows the witness, never precedes it.\n\nTHE ROSTER IS NOT TRANSCRIBED HERE. This row previously carried a hand survey — 'two of six startable roots are bindable' plus the four names and why each lacked a validation — and it rotted exactly as a hand-maintained count must: two of the four it named (v1-test-hygiene-producer-retirement, pderive-typesafe-nullary-reflection) were accepted while the sentence still listed them as unbound, and the leading count was edited twice by unrelated changes. startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract() computes the same set from the nodes themselves, so read it there and delete nothing by hand. What stays here is the RULE, which does not change when the roster does.\n\nv1-test-migration records both sides of that rule: its false-green deletion-guard contract was removed, then a behavior-grain validation that can fail on zero progress was authored and bound. See v1_test_migration_binding_history_note for that history and v1_test_migration_contract_note for the current completion authority."

