data v1_seed_honesty_decision_contract_note: String = "The validation names v1-seed-honesty-decision and executes the node's whole handback. Feeding one artifact identity as both claim and check refuses with bootstrap_self_verify_same_artifact; feeding separately identified artifacts with planted divergent content refuses with bootstrap_self_verify_diverged; mismatched source/compiler/hash-pin provenance refuses with bootstrap_self_verify_provenance_mismatch; separately identified artifacts with equal content and equal provenance admit, proving the refusals are discriminating rather than unconditional. The final conjunct requires seed_honesty_decision to carry one of the two operator verdicts whose rationale is NonEmptyStr by construction. That conjunct was SeedHonestyUndecided until 2026-08-02, so the closing validation was deliberately red and enrolled QuarantineProbeExpectRed and could not report done over the missing half; the operator verdict FixedPointTrustSufficient greened it and the enrollment was deleted in the same change (see v1_seed_honesty_decision_acceptance_note). The undecided pole did NOT retire with the quarantine: the conjunction is now a function of the decision, so seed_honesty_undecided_would_keep_closing_contract_red still executes it against SeedHonestyUndecided and reds if that arm is ever weakened. PRODUCTION-WIRING TRIGGER, STILL OPEN AND NOT DISCHARGED BY THE VERDICT: the realized-comparison and regen paths do not yet route their artifact identities through seed_self_verify_check. This contract proves the decision procedure and gates completion, but it is not yet the live trust-path authority. The verdict took the second branch of the choice this sentence set out -- fixed-point trust ruled sufficient -- and the rationale therefore disposes of the unwired path explicitly rather than implying it is protected, alongside the two other residuals it accepts (a fixed point does not defeat a self-reproducing defect; rollback is not fix-forward). Routing the realized artifacts through this check belongs to the generation-admission program, which is unbuilt. The full independent rebuild remains out of scope here."

data v1_test_migration_binding_history_note: String = "v1-test-migration WAS DELIBERATELY UNBOUND after its former binding to test_migration_delete_guard_holds was removed: that binding produced a false green, as reading what the guard actually computes showed.\n\nThe guard is DIFF-SCOPED. cli_run.rs test_migration_delete_guard_uncovered_deletes_inner resolves a (base, head) pair, returns an empty violation list when base_rev == head_rev, returns an empty list again when neither rev resolves and GUNBC_CI_DIFF_BASE is unset, and otherwise inspects only the v1 test paths DELETED between those revs. Violations are empty whenever a change deletes no uncovered legacy test file, so the guard answers true. Measured on the tree that carried it: the guard passes today while test_migration_debt_live_module_count() is 81, so a dispatched agent could do no migration work at all and Verify would still go green. A validation that cannot fail on zero progress cannot close a node (DESIGN 5 — a wrong answer must be loud, and 'done' asserted over undone work is exactly the silent wrong answer).\n\nThe guard was KEPT at the time this note was written, as a per-change deletion wall. IT IS NOW DELETED (2026-08-12, V1-CONSUMER-DRAIN-1): test_migration_delete_guard_holds, test_migration_delete_guard_uncovered_deletes and the whole guard body are gone from cli_run.rs, from the primitive registry, from the interpreter dispatch surface and from the v1 type env. The reason is not that the analysis above was wrong — it was right, and it is why the node was unbound — but that the guard's SUBJECT stopped existing: it authorized deletions by checking a stem against _retired.dag / _retained.dag carriers, and gunbc#8146 deleted every such carrier when the authority inverted from classifying departures to declaring residents. A per-change wall over an empty authorize-set refuses nothing. The deletion wall it provided is SUPERSEDED, not dropped: an undeclared or stale module now reds through the identity join in v2.lens.test_migration_debt retained_rust_kernel_wall_holds, which does not depend on anyone remembering to classify a departure. The paragraphs above are retained as the reasoning that unbound the node, not as a description of live code.\n\nThe node's own bar — 'the per-file coverage mapping with nothing unmatched' — required a closing validation at the grain of legacy test BEHAVIOR, not modules: an admitted typed disposition for every discovered legacy test function, with unclassified as the derived set difference reaching zero. The module count is a trend metric that overstates debt by construction, because classification is exact-stem filename matching (chosen fail-closed after fuzzy matching wrongly reported an unrelated witness as covering `pipeline`), so a real witness under a different name still reads as debt. That missing graph once made the node honestly ClosingContractMissing in startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract. It now exists and the node is bound to it by v1_test_migration_execution_contract; v1_test_migration_contract_note records why that validation, rather than the deletion guard, is the completion authority."

fn v1_test_migration_execution_contract() -> WorkItemExecutionContract {
  gunbc_claim_execution_contract(
    entry: "src/v2/test/claim/manual/test_migration_debt_test.dag" as FilePath,
    function: "retained_rust_kernel_wall_holds_against_live_tree" as NonEmptyStr,
  )
}

data roadmap_receipt_continuity_execution_contract_note: String = "THE CLOSING CHECK FOR roadmap-receipt-continuity. Acceptance history is append-only JSONL carrier dag/gunbc/roadmap_acceptance_event_history.jsonl; roadmap_acceptance_receipts is a replay projection only. Authored seal count/digest are deleted. Merge-base prior_history parses carrier content via git.Core.Show — never prior_history = [] on a changed carrier (PriorHistoryRevisionEvaluationRequired dissolved). The hermetic contract plants each operator red control: deleted events, prefix law on non-empty observed prior, empty-prior bypass discriminant (proves the old authority-changed path would admit deletion), digest-omitted field mutation, wrong prior digest, revocation while live, duplicate revocations, exact revocation admit, criteria rewrite, git-refusal observation, and typed load/projection refusal chain. Live git-observed integrity executes in test.claim.roadmap_receipt_continuity_live_witness (ReadsLiveTree, bin_witness_wet_entries)."

fn roadmap_receipt_continuity_execution_contract() -> WorkItemExecutionContract {
  gunbc_claim_execution_contract(
    entry: "dag/test/claim/roadmap_receipt_continuity_acceptance_test.dag" as FilePath,
    function: "roadmap_receipt_continuity_acceptance_contract_holds" as NonEmptyStr,
  )
}

data v1_test_migration_contract_note: String = "THE RETENTION-KERNEL CLOSING DISCRIMINATOR. REBOUND 2026-08-11 (gunbc#8146, review 51209) from legacy_test_behavior_unclassified_frontier_is_zero, which bound this node to the BEHAVIOR-grain disposition graph. That model is retired: the cutover inverted the authority from classifying departures to declaring residents (test.retirement.model retained_legacy_rust_test_modules), and legacy_test_behavior_dispositions() is now empty, so the old contract could never green under the model that replaced it. Leaving it bound would have left TWO authorities for one population (DESIGN section 3) and pointed downstream work at a completion criterion that no longer exists. The node now binds retained_rust_kernel_wall_holds_against_live_tree: a two-direction identity join between the live src/v1/tests/src discovery scan and the declared retained roster, plus the execution check (no #[ignore] / #[should_panic] in a retained module, default libtest harness). It does NOT bind the diff-scoped delete guard, which was deleted with the ratchets it served. Rung, stated rather than implied: the identity join is a construction wall (an undeclared module or a stale row is unwritable-and-green), the execution half is mechanically preventive text scanning with its false-negative class named at the lens."


fn with_execution(rn: RoadmapNode, e: WorkItemExecutionContract) -> RoadmapNode {
  RoadmapNode {
    identity: rn.identity,
    node: rn.node,
    line: rn.line,
    carriers: rn.carriers,
    sizing: rn.sizing,
    execution: e,
    owner: rn.owner,
    signoff: rn.signoff,
  }
}

fn sign(rn: RoadmapNode, s: Signoff) -> RoadmapNode {
  RoadmapNode {
    identity: rn.identity,
    node: rn.node,
    line: rn.line,
    carriers: rn.carriers,
    sizing: rn.sizing,
    execution: rn.execution,
    owner: rn.owner,
    signoff: s,
  }
}

fn carrier(path: String) -> List<Pointer> {
  [Pointer { label: "authority", path: path }]
}

fn fields(
  headline: String,
  boundary: String,
  displaced_cost: String,
  first_slice: String,
  red_control: String,
  out_of_scope: String,
  handback: String
) -> TicketFields {
  TicketFields {
    headline: headline,
    brief: boundary,
    displaced_cost: displaced_cost,
    current_state: concat("Unaccepted; next executable slice: ", first_slice),
    first_slice: first_slice,
    red_control: red_control,
    out_of_scope: out_of_scope,
    handback: handback,
    updates: [],
    authored_on: "",
    last_verified_on: "",
  }
}

fn active_sized(
  identity: String,
  id: String,
  owner: String,
  path: String,
  intricacy: Intricacy,
  volume: Volume,
  t: TicketFields
) -> RoadmapNode {
  RoadmapNode {
    identity: identity as RoadmapNodeIdentity,
    node: nid(s: id),
    line: TicketLine { done: false, ticket: t },
    carriers: carrier(path: path),
    sizing: Sized {
      intricacy: intricacy,
      volume: volume,
      repo: "gunbc",
      acceptance: ManualAcceptance,
    },
    execution: ExecutionContractUnspecified,
    owner: owner,
    signoff: Unsigned,
  }
}

fn active(identity: String,
  id: String, owner: String, path: String, t: TicketFields) -> RoadmapNode {
  active_sized(
    identity: identity,
    id: id,
    owner: owner,
    path: path,
    intricacy: IntricacyMedium,
    volume: VolumeMedium,
    t: t,
  )
}

fn active_small(identity: String,
  id: String, owner: String, path: String, t: TicketFields) -> RoadmapNode {
  active_sized(
    identity: identity,
    id: id,
    owner: owner,
    path: path,
    intricacy: IntricacyLow,
    volume: VolumeSmall,
    t: t,
  )
}

fn active_large(identity: String,
  id: String, owner: String, path: String, t: TicketFields) -> RoadmapNode {
  active_sized(
    identity: identity,
    id: id,
    owner: owner,
    path: path,
    intricacy: IntricacyHigh,
    volume: VolumeLarge,
    t: t,
  )
}

fn edge(child: String, parent: String) -> RoadmapEdge {
  RoadmapEdge { child: nid(s: child), parent: nid(s: parent) }
}

fn observation_scoped_run_seed_growth_justification() -> SeedGrowthJustification {
  SeedGrowthJustification {
    hand_authored_declarations: [
      DeclarationRef {
        module_path: "v1_compiler.cli_run",
        decl_name: "handle_run_with_options",
        field: WholeDeclaration
      },
      DeclarationRef {
        module_path: "v1_compiler.cli_run",
        decl_name: "capture_scoped_run_stderr",
        field: WholeDeclaration
      },
      DeclarationRef {
        module_path: "v1_compiler.cli_run",
        decl_name: "replay_scoped_run_stderr",
        field: WholeDeclaration
      }
    ],
    reason: "Showing progress while a command runs has to reach the point where the old runner starts a program. It needs to know which piece of code was chosen, hold back error output until it can be shown in the right order, and report exactly one ending — and the rules for all of that live in the substrate, not in Rust.",
    owning_dissolution_lane: nid(s: "v1-hand-queue-drain"),
    trigger: "Move this reach-through into a described operation, then hollow out the corresponding part of the command-line tool so generated code does the work; the hand-written bridge is deleted at that point.",
    current_boundary: "dag/gunbc/observation_seed_render.dag; dag/test/claim/observation_seed_scoped_run_witness_test.dag; src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs; src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs; src/v1/stage0/tests/scoped_run_observation.rs",
  }
}

fn seed_growth_fields(j: SeedGrowthJustification) -> TicketFields {
  fields(
    headline: "Account for the five hand-written files that progress output still needs",
    boundary: concat(
      "Showing progress while a command runs still relies on hand-written code, in exactly these five files and no others: ",
      j.current_boundary,
    ),
    displaced_cost: concat("Why it is needed: ", j.reason),
    first_slice: concat(
      "Hand the job of deleting these files to ",
      concat(j.owning_dissolution_lane as String, ", without adding a sixth file."),
    ),
    red_control: concat(
      "Any new hand-written code for this outside those five files, or without this plan, is refused. The plan: ",
      j.trigger,
    ),
    out_of_scope: "Not a general list of hand-written files, not new progress features, and not a status report.",
    handback: concat(
      "Show that the five files are still the only ones, and carry this exact plan into ",
      j.owning_dissolution_lane as String,
    ),
  )
}

fn declared_roadmap_nodes() -> List<RoadmapNode> {
  concat([
    active_sized(
      identity: "rn_44VKSHNX2ZNTDRCPGGNSFJWCWN",
      id: "2-scm-git-upstream-model",
      owner: "source-intent",
      path: "docs/plans/dag-scm-design.md",
      intricacy: IntricacyHigh,
      volume: VolumeMedium,
      t: fields(
        headline: "Write down how Git really stores things",
        boundary: "Describe Git on its own terms: files, folders, commits, tags, file permissions, symlinks, links to other repositories, and how it names and finds them. Prove it by reading a small repository and writing it back unchanged.",
        displaced_cost: "If we invent a one-size-fits-all model first, it quietly drops things like executable permissions and symlinks — and every tool built on top inherits the loss.",
        first_slice: "Finish the storage description, then read one small repository and write it back unchanged.",
        red_control: "A plain file, an executable file, a symlink, a link to another repository, and a tag all stay distinguishable; writing against an out-of-date starting point refuses instead of overwriting; a missing object never reads as empty.",
        out_of_scope: "No shared source-control interface, no hosting or server work, and no guessing that a renamed file is the same file.",
        handback: "The model with citations to Git's own docs, the read/write-back receipts, the refusal receipt, and a list of anything we still cannot represent."
      )
    ),
    active_sized(
      identity: "rn_5JX128MQ9XYR760B5CFC3VECTN",
      id: "2-scm-mercurial-upstream-model",
      owner: "source-intent",
      path: "docs/plans/dag-scm-design.md",
      intricacy: IntricacyHigh,
      volume: VolumeMedium,
      t: fields(
        headline: "Write down how Mercurial really works, in its own words",
        boundary: "Describe Mercurial from its own documentation: its permanent change IDs versus the numbers local to one copy, its branches, bookmarks, tags, and its draft/published states. Use no Git vocabulary.",
        displaced_cost: "Mercurial's ideas are not Git's. Renaming them into Git words loses the differences, and a later shared design would be built on a mistranslation.",
        first_slice: "Write the description and read one small repository back out, leaving anything we cannot safely write marked unavailable.",
        red_control: "A copy-local revision number can change while the permanent change ID does not; draft, published, and hidden stay three different things; a bookmark, a named branch, and a branch tip cannot collapse into one idea.",
        out_of_scope: "No Mercurial support in the product, no shared source-control interface, and no faking missing facts from timestamps.",
        handback: "The description with citations, the read-back receipt, an explicit list of what we cannot do yet, and the differences that must survive into any later shared design."
      )
    ),
