    active_large(
      identity: "rn_6BXPK1RV9ZTNQ3WD8HGYM40CFJ",
      id: "placement-compile-pool-envelope",
      owner: "ci-placement",
      path: "docs/plans/srvn-buildcache-provisioning-design.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "Derive the process, compiler and memory limits from one envelope per machine",
        boundary: "Shared compilation runs in its own managed place rather than inside whichever job slot happened to start it. How many processes a slot may hold, how wide the compiler may fan out, and how much memory the shared pool may take are all derived from one declared envelope for that machine instead of being three numbers anyone can set independently. Every limit is read back as the machine actually holds it.",
        displaced_cost: "The immediate outage — jobs unable to start processes against a cap far below the packaged one — and the fault behind it, where every machine's cache-miss compiles are charged to one job slot's budget and on one machine a retired slot's, are both consequences of these limits being separately writable. Two distinct failure kinds were charged to whatever code happened to be under test for a whole day: jobs dying unable to start a process at all, and jobs killed from an outer budget while sitting far under their own — across four independent measurements at 8.8 percent of the visible limit, with the peak identical on every attempt. Neither is a defect in any change under review, and both arrive looking exactly like one.",
        first_slice: "Restore the affected machine's process limit and read back the value the kernel actually holds, then move shared compilation into its own place with a stated admission bound.",
        red_control: "The acceptance classes are typed and separately named, never one generic death: a process-count exhaustion reports ProcessAdmissionExhausted; a kill imposed from an outer budget reports OuterResourceEnvelopeKilled, and the two must reproduce as two different things; a command that exits successfully while the machine's effective limit is unchanged reports EffectiveLimitMismatch — a failed read-back, not a success; a cache server whose place cannot be established reports CompilePoolPlacementRefused rather than being assumed to be ours. A pool whose effective limits differ from the envelope refuses. Widening the compiler's fan-out past the envelope refuses. An envelope that grows because more work arrived, rather than because the budget was raised deliberately, refuses.",
        out_of_scope: "A larger machine or a raised memory cap is not the remedy — the envelope is fixed and the mechanism fits inside it. Not routing jobs between machines, which the rows above own.",
        handback: "The derived envelope, the read-back limits from every machine, and the previously failing build run again."
      )
    ),

    active_large(
      identity: "rn_7KWZQ2VBNM4XCT0RDPLHGJ86Y1",
      id: "compute-exact-work-contract",
      owner: "compute",
      path: "docs/plans/execution-spine-design.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "One way to ask for work to be done, with the subject pinned exactly",
        boundary: "A request names the exact tree it applies to, the operation, the inputs it declares and the outputs it promises, the toolchain and environment it needs, its resource envelope, and which providers may serve it. The answer is one of a fixed set of endings: succeeded, failed, refused by the infrastructure, cancelled, or refused because what came back did not match the outputs declared. A branch name or a working directory is not a subject.",
        displaced_cost: "Building, checking, regenerating, changing machines, serving models and eventually judging changes are each growing a separate way of asking for work to be done. Four execution systems means four queues, four retry rules, four ideas of what success means, and nowhere to say where a piece of work actually ran.",
        first_slice: "The request and outcome shapes with one provider behind them, serving one real operation from end to end. FIRST DAILY CONSUMER (operator, 2026-08-08): dirty-worktree verification — an exact dirty-tree snapshot runs all affected (or explicitly conservatively complete) v2 tests and returns an exact receipt in at most five seconds on the normal warm path, with a distinct BaseUnstable answer when the base layer itself is red or unverified. It consumes this contract, CI2's reusable execute_v2_witness_plan operation, and optionally confidence's narrowing — confidence is optional for narrowing, never for correctness: a partial impact answer selects the complete corpus explicitly, never a skip and never v1.",
        red_control: "A request whose subject is a moving reference refuses. A provider that cannot serve the requested class of operation refuses before running rather than quietly answering a smaller question. The five endings stay five — a machine that killed the work cannot render as the work having failed.",
        out_of_scope: "Not the handing back of what was produced, which is the row below. Not changing what any existing command computes. Not a new scheduler beside the realization spine this builds on.",
        handback: "The request and outcome carriers, one provider behind them, and one real operation with all five endings demonstrated."
      )
    ),
    active_large(
      identity: "rn_1MDQX8ZTVK5NBW3RCPHJLG024Y",
      id: "compute-artifact-return-and-materialization",
      owner: "compute",
      path: "docs/plans/execution-spine-design.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "Work that cannot hand back what it produced has not succeeded",
        boundary: "Every output a computation declared comes back identified by its content, with its size and what produced it, through a manifest the caller can read. Whether those bytes are then copied into a working tree is a separate and explicit step. Work that produces files may only run where its outputs can be returned; a provider that cannot return them offers checking only, and refuses producing work before it starts rather than after.",
        displaced_cost: "A remote run finished successfully and produced nothing locally; the stale binary left behind was then compared against itself and reported no difference. Every remote build in this environment currently has that shape, and it is silent.",
        first_slice: "Return the declared outputs of one producing operation by content identity, and make the same operation refuse on a provider that cannot return them.",
        red_control: "A run reporting success while a declared output is absent refuses instead. An output whose content does not match what the manifest claims refuses. A provider unable to return outputs refuses a producing request rather than answering it with a verdict.",
        out_of_scope: "Not a distributed content store — exact subject binding, durability, retrieval and refusal come first. Not work that changes a machine, which needs a lease and a read-back rather than an artefact.",
        handback: "The manifest with content identities, one materialisation into a working tree, and the three refusals demonstrated."
      )
    ),

    active_large(
      identity: "rn_5PZBW1KVNT7XCQ3RDMHJLG0946",
      id: "compute-deduplication-and-admission",
      owner: "compute",
      path: "docs/plans/execution-spine-design.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "Identical work runs once and is handed to everyone who asked for it",
        boundary: "Two requests naming the same computation are the same work: one producer runs and everyone else attaches to its result as a consumer. The fabric decides what may run — how many cold builds a machine carries at once, what the compile pool's total demand may be, which host serves a request, and what order competing work is served in — and tells a caller which of those it is waiting on. A caller never starts something merely because it cannot tell whether somebody else is already producing it.",
        displaced_cost: "Ten agents needing the same binary for the same tree currently means ten builds, because nothing lets one of them discover the other nine. Moving the present arrangement onto our own machines WITHOUT this multiplies the load instead of sharing it, which is why this row comes before more consumers rather than after them.",
        first_slice: "Two agent sessions ask for the same artefact-producing build of the same tree: one producer runs, one compiler build happens, one output is returned, and both attach to it. Deliberately NOT a read-only check — collapsing two verdicts would demonstrate the mechanism on the one case where duplicate work costs nothing, while the case that swamps the fleet is two cold builds.",
        red_control: "Two identical requests produce one execution and both callers receive the same result, with the same output identity. Two requests differing anywhere in the identity — tree, toolchain, target, profile, features, environment — must NOT collapse, each difference proven separately. A caller refused for want of capacity is told so, and that refusal is counted rather than becoming a longer queue nobody can see.",
        out_of_scope: "Not what any operation computes. Not the handing back of outputs, which is its sibling. Not provisioning the machines the pool runs on.",
        handback: "One producer serving several consumers on a real operation, the non-collapse controls, the standing a caller sees, and agents no longer launching duplicate cold builds."
      )
    ),

    active_large(
      identity: "rn_0LNVJ4CQ8ZRHT2XBWD6PGK9YFS",
      id: "ci-owned-execution",
      owner: "ci-control",
      path: "docs/plans/ci-process-end-to-end.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "Run the required checks on our own machines, from a subject we hold ourselves",
        boundary: "Seeing a new commit creates a durable piece of work of our own, identified by the exact base, head, and combined commit under test. One machine holds it under a lease for the whole run, and the run ends in one typed result we keep. Starting, queueing and handing out the work involve no third-party workflow and no registered runner. The old floor is quarry and shadow oracle, not the template: obligations port only when a disagreement proves they carry a real property, and its batches, ordering, settlement and status vocabulary are not inherited.",
        displaced_cost: "Starting, queueing, handing out, cancelling and reporting all belong to someone else today. When their side has a bad day our machines wedge, their re-runs test a stale combination, and every one of those failures arrives looking like a fault in our own code.",
        first_slice: "One exact subject, one bounded population, the emitted native bundle, owned execution, one typed receipt — with the old path running as shadow beside it rather than as the thing being replicated.",
        red_control: "Restarting the controller recovers unfinished work. The same subject does not run twice at once. Asking for a re-run builds a fresh combination against current main rather than replaying an old one. Losing the machine mid-run returns the work instead of stranding it. A disagreement between the owned path and the shadow is a counted finding, never silently resolved in either direction.",
        out_of_scope: "GitHub stays the repository and the place people review. Porting old-floor obligations wholesale is out — each ports only on a proven disagreement.",
        handback: "The durable queue, one run held under a lease from start to finish, and its result carrying the exact subject it tested."
      )
    ),
    active(
      identity: "rn_8XBLW9DKZH5NCQ0RTVPMGJ4F2",
      id: "ci-executed-coverage-receipt",
      owner: "ci-control",
      path: "docs/plans/ci-process-end-to-end.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "A check reports what it actually executed, not merely that it ran",
        boundary: "Every required check carries the population it executed — which witnesses, which corpus, how many — and a run that executed nothing cannot report a verdict of any kind. A build failure that stops the corpus from running is its own outcome, distinct from the corpus running and disagreeing.",
        displaced_cost: "A scheduled check spent days reporting failures while executing no corpus at all, because a step ahead of it failed and the result looked exactly like a real disagreement. Nobody could tell the difference from outside, so a genuine signal was ignored for days.",
        first_slice: "Carry the executed population on one required check, and refuse a verdict when that population is empty.",
        red_control: "A run whose executed population is zero refuses rather than reporting either pass or fail. A failure ahead of the corpus is a different outcome from a corpus disagreement. A check that cannot say what it executed does not count as having executed.",
        out_of_scope: "Not what the corpus contains or how it is selected, and not per-witness enrolment — only that a verdict names the work that produced it.",
        handback: "One required check carrying its executed population, with the empty-population refusal and the distinct upstream-failure outcome both demonstrated."
      )
    ),
    active_small(
      identity: "rn_W86W65EBR3HW41MK0X3RBMJ8BT",
      id: "ci-gate-contention-independent-verdict",
      owner: "ci-control",
      path: "docs/plans/ci-performance-tanking-evidence.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "Host load cannot decide a semantic merge verdict",
        boundary: "A check that blocks merging decides something about the change. The semantic verdict derives from the executed assertion; a timeout or resource exhaustion is a distinct execution outcome, never projected into assertion failure, and never absorbed by widening the cutoff. Work that cannot fit its budget is decomposed or moved through the correct execution mechanism — the cutoff is not raised to admit it.",
        displaced_cost: "One check was measured ten times across four separate efforts with nothing in the code changing between them, and the readings ranged from well under its limit to well over it. It blocked one change outright, produced a false alarm on another whose own checks all passed, and sent three separate efforts hunting for a defect that was never there — an unchanged semantic subject passing or failing on host contention.",
        first_slice: "On the one gate currently deciding merges on machine load, split the outcome: the same unchanged subject retains the same semantic result across repeated runs, and an over-budget run reports ExecutionTimedOut or CoverageRefused rather than a failed assertion.",
        red_control: "Repeated runs of the same unchanged code land on the same semantic side. A timeout reports as ExecutionTimedOut or CoverageRefused, never as an assertion failure. Raising the limit, or moving the check somewhere it stops running per change, refuses as a way of closing this. A quiet-host pass is not accepted as the only evidence.",
        out_of_scope: "Not a general performance effort, and not a review of every limit — only gates currently deciding merges on machine load. The decomposition or migration of the slow work itself belongs to the execution rows.",
        handback: "The same unchanged subject holding one semantic result across repeated contended runs, the over-budget outcome demonstrated as its own typed ending, and the limit itself untouched."
      )
    ),
    active(
      identity: "rn_3HKRW7TBZP1MQ5NDXV0GCJ8YLA",
      id: "ci-check-projection",
      owner: "ci-control",
      path: "docs/plans/ci-process-end-to-end.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "The result shown on GitHub is a copy of ours, never the original",
        boundary: "Our stored result is published as the required check. No check present means not yet observed and never counts as satisfied. Cancelled, timed out, refused by the infrastructure, and genuinely failed stay four different things the whole way through. A queued merge is checked against the exact commit that queue built. If GitHub cannot be reached the publishing waits; the result itself is never lost.",
        displaced_cost: "NO LONGER A PREDICTION — three measured receipts, and the skew runs BOTH WAYS, so no single sign-correction fixes it. #7932 at 493de348 (2026-08-07): a CANCELLED required ci folded into PASS and the tally reported MERGE CRITERIA MET while GitHub held mergeStateStatus=BLOCKED. #8051 at b4a8d5d: two runs on one head, and the concurrency-cancelled one pinned checks_state at PENDING beside a SUCCESS row. #8059 (2026-08-10) is what that costs when the runners are also down — it merged on a cancelled run carrying a carrier rename whose call sites another merged PR had just added, putting 99 mechanically-detectable type errors on main across two files (18 SubstrateLongLaneRow fields, 81 uses of a HasTrigger constructor defined nowhere), so main could not derive its own generated artifacts and every PR adding an emitted module was blocked outright. Three sessions each found it believing their own branch was broken, and two independently wrote the same 81-row repair within a minute. A related trap on the same surface: mergeable: MERGEABLE is GitHub's conflict-freedom field, not a checks verdict, so it is never a second confirmation. None of this is a review gap and none of it is a review fix — every one of the 99 is a type error the compiler decides in milliseconds; the only thing that had to happen was for the checks to run.",
        first_slice: "Publish one required check from a stored result, with the four endings kept apart.",
        red_control: "An absent check does not admit a merge. A generic failure that erases a cancellation refuses. Publishing against a commit other than the one that ran refuses. Both measured skew directions are controls, not one: a cancelled row admitted as pass refuses, AND a cancelled row that pins an otherwise-settled head at pending refuses — a fix asserted for one direction without an executed control for the other is incomplete. The verdict derives from the authoritative merge state, never from aggregating rollup rows, and conflict-freedom is never read as a checks verdict.",
        out_of_scope: "No change to what the branch requires until both paths have been seen to agree.",
        handback: "One required check published from a stored result, and the four endings demonstrated separately."
      )
    ),
    active(
      identity: "rn_8YMCT2GQVZ0KRB6WNP4XHDJ71L",
      id: "ci-actions-runner-retirement",
      owner: "ci-control",
      path: "docs/plans/ci-process-end-to-end.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "Remove the hosted-runner path once we carry the required checks",
        boundary: "With our own path publishing the required checks, the workflow, the runner registrations and the recovery timer are removed together in one change, so there is never a period in which two things can hand out the same work.",
        displaced_cost: "Retires the vendor's dispatcher as something our fleet's health depends on — the reason the recovery timer has to exist at all, and the reason a bad day on their side becomes a wedged fleet on ours.",
        first_slice: "Run both paths over the same subjects and compare the verdicts before removing anything.",
        red_control: "A disagreement on any subject blocks removal. A half-removed state in which both paths can still hand out work is rejected.",
        out_of_scope: "GitHub keeps the repository, the review surface and the check display. This is about who starts and runs the work, not where it is discussed.",
        handback: "Agreement across a real run of subjects, then one change that removes the old path."
      )
    ),
    active_small(
      identity: "rn_4QGZD9NWX3TVB7LMKR0PHY62CS",
      id: "ci-remote-build-containment",
      owner: "ci-control",
      path: "docs/plans/ci-process-end-to-end.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "Contain the remote build wrapper to its safe subset, then retire it into the compute contract",
        boundary: "This row is a migration, not a home. Until outputs can be handed back, the wrapper admits exactly one remote mode — checking something without producing anything the local tree then needs — and refuses any invocation that would write. Every result names the source it checked and the executable it checked with, so a stale local artefact can never stand in for a remote answer. When the compute contract can return outputs, the wrapper's remaining useful behaviour becomes one provider behind it and the wrapper is deleted rather than extended.",
        displaced_cost: "There is no stated rule for when this wrapper is the right thing, so it gets guessed at; a remote run meant to produce files can finish successfully having produced nothing locally, and that has already been consumed as a passing check. Every day it stays unclassified, more work is authored against a boundary that means two contradictory things.",
        first_slice: "Classify the wrapper's operations and refuse the remote writing class before dispatch, not after.",
        red_control: "A remote invocation declared read-only that writes refuses. A check whose local artefact is older than the source it claims to have checked refuses. A producing request must refuse rather than silently becoming a verdict-only one.",
        out_of_scope: "This is not where remote execution lives — the compute rows above own that. No rewrite of the wrapper and no new capability added to it; the only additions permitted here are refusals.",
        handback: "The classification, the refusing controls, and the wrapper deleted once its behaviour is a provider behind the compute contract."
      )
    ),

    active(
      identity: "rn_0PJXVP9KNDXZFHXMDM9XQ7TTSX",
      id: "commit-writer-admission",
      owner: "generated-artifact",
      path: "docs/plans/generated-file-conflict-policy.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "One admission predicate that every commit writer must pass before it may write",
        boundary: "Admission consumes a complete staged-index observation whose population must equal the exact staged index, never a caller-selected list, with two independent refusal arms: any unmerged index stage refuses typed and located with the affected path, and the staged blobs are inspected for the complete conflict-marker grammar, so a file collapsed to stage zero with markers inside refuses. Text classification is observed, never caller-declared; a fixture exemption is a provenance receipt bound to the same staged path and blob, never a path allowlist. The pre-commit hook stays fast local feedback; the predicate at the writer's boundary is the enforcement.",
        displaced_cost: "An automated writer has already committed and pushed literal conflict-marker blocks to main by committing through an unresolved merge, and the refusing merge driver raised that class only to mechanically preventable — the repository's own config authority states that commit-writer admission remains the boundary a writer cannot pass. Until this lane lands, the other lanes shrink how many files can be caught by textual adjacency while every writer stays one absorbing fallback away from repeating the incident on the files that remain.",
        first_slice: "The predicate with the unmerged-index refusal, consumed by one real writer, with the historical incident's exact shape reproduced as the discriminating red.",
        red_control: "A merge with unmerged index entries refuses typed and located; the staged-marker-text variant refuses separately; an observation population that differs from the staged index refuses in both directions; a freely constructed provenance value whose path happens to match a staged file refuses; a clean tree admits.",
        out_of_scope: "The infrastructure-side daemon binding is reported for operator routing, not landed from this repository. No repo-wide raw text scan, and no new stringly conflicted-tree fact — the Git model already carries the index-stage and diff-status facts this consumes.",
        handback: "The predicate consumed by every repo-lane commit writer, the incident witness pair red then green, and the two writer bindings carried as countable work rows rather than prose."
      )
    ),
    active(
      identity: "rn_7VNBH0KXR5ZQWD2PLGT3MJ86CY",
      id: "generated-projection-registry-containment",
      owner: "generated-artifact",
      path: "docs/plans/generated-file-conflict-policy.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "Stop writing the whole file registry into a generated file",
        boundary: "A generated artefact must not carry the full list of generated paths. The workflow asks Git which of the paths that actually conflicted are generated, reading that classification from both sides' committed state rather than from a list baked into the workflow text. Adding a generated file then changes the classification projection and leaves the workflow's own bytes alone.",
        displaced_cost: "One enormous line holding every generated path means any change to that population rewrites the same spot in a shared file, so two branches conflict over a list neither of them disagrees about. That has happened repeatedly, and each occurrence costs a manual resolution carrying no information.",
        first_slice: "Take the path population out of the workflow and classify only the paths that actually conflicted.",
        red_control: "Adding or removing a generated file leaves the workflow byte-identical. A path classified as generated on only one side refuses rather than being taken as generated. A conflict that also touches hand-written files refuses before resolving anything. Paths containing spaces or newlines survive. A conflicted classification file sitting in the working tree does not get to decide the classification.",
        out_of_scope: "The rule that generated files are regenerated rather than merged by hand does not change; this changes how a generated path is recognised, not what happens to one.",
        handback: "The population gone from the workflow, the previously conflicting case no longer conflicting, and each refusal demonstrated separately."
      )
    ),
    active(
      identity: "rn_1CJXM6RTPZ8VKQ0NDWB4HG5YFL",
      id: "generated-commit-policy-census",
      owner: "generated-artifact",
      path: "docs/plans/generated-file-conflict-policy.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "Decide which generated files need to be in the repository at all",
