    active_large(
      identity: "rn_5ZKQW8BXTN2VRD0MHJ7GPL34CY",
      id: "fleet-main-revision-authority",
      owner: "fleet",
      path: "docs/plans/fleet-self-converge-enforcement-design.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "A merge to main is the instruction, and nothing else applies it",
        boundary: "The state the machines should be in is the exact revision merged to main, held durably by whatever is applying it. A newer revision replaces an older one, an older or unrelated one refuses, and the same one again does nothing. The instruction survives a restart of the thing applying it and an outage at the hosting provider — an event from them may make it happen sooner but is never the only place the instruction lives. A machine that was unreachable or refused is tried again against the revision still held, with no new commit needed.",
        displaced_cost: "Every fleet-touching merge currently ends with the same question — is that actually on the machines — and the only way to answer it is to go and look. Somebody has to remember to apply things, and forgetting is invisible until it causes an incident.",
        first_slice: "Watch main directly, hold one durable revision, and show it survives a restart of the applier.",
        red_control: "An older revision does not overwrite a newer one, and two arriving close together do not finish out of order and leave a machine behind. An unrelated history refuses. A missed notification still converges on the next pass. The same revision again performs no changes at all.",
        out_of_scope: "Not how any individual setting is applied — the closed loop above owns that. Not the fleet-wide answer, which is its sibling row.",
        handback: "The held revision surviving a restart, the ordering refusals, and a machine that was down converging on its return without anyone committing anything."
      )
    ),
    active(
      identity: "rn_2WPGT6NCZQ9XKBR1DVLM40HJY8",
      id: "fleet-atomic-convergence-verdict",
      owner: "fleet",
      path: "docs/plans/fleet-acceptance-criteria.md",
      t: fields(
        headline: "One answer for the whole fleet, or exactly which machines are not there yet",
        boundary: "Each machine produces a receipt — what was wanted, what was there, what was planned, what was applied, and what was read back afterwards. Those fold into a single answer for the fleet, joined with the lifecycle cells enrollment constructs: every required host-and-phase cell is observed, refused, unreachable, drifted, or converged, and a required cell nobody has observed prevents convergence rather than defaulting green. Anything short of all of them is not converged, with the machines that refused, could not be reached, or have drifted each named separately.",
        displaced_cost: "One machine can hold the new value while the others hold the old one and nothing anywhere says so, which is exactly why the honest answer to whether something is applied is currently to go and check by hand. And the fleet phase matrix currently has exactly one real observation producer cell — one host, one phase, hard-coded — so every other lifecycle claim in the fleet is unobserved by construction; enrollment makes obligations countable, not satisfied.",
        first_slice: "Fold the existing per-machine receipts into one fleet answer for a single narrow setting.",
        red_control: "One machine short of the roster reports not converged, never converged with a note attached. Unreachable, refused and drifted stay three different answers, and a machine we could not reach is never simply missing from the count. A command that succeeded while the real value did not change reads as a failed read-back.",
        out_of_scope: "No new ways of changing machines are added here — this row is the answer, not the action.",
        handback: "The fleet answer with all four machine outcomes demonstrated."
      )
    ),
