data criteria_digest_reword_note: String = "Both digests below moved on 2026-07-29 because every row was rewritten in plain language, and the digest covers the brief, the pass/fail test, and what gets handed back. Worth being blunt about the limit this exposes: the digest cannot tell a rewording from a genuine change to the bar — it only knows the words moved. Nothing about what these two rows had to prove changed, and the evidence each names is the same run and the same files as before, so re-pinning is correct here. But that judgement was a person's, not the mechanism's, and a reviewer should treat any digest change as a prompt to re-read the row rather than as a formality. This is the same gap roadmap_acceptance_receipt_note already names: until a row's pass/fail test points at the test that discharges it, the link between words and evidence is asserted rather than derived."

data entry_graph_union_construction_acceptance_note: String = "CLOSED NO-GO (operator adjudication 2026-08-01). Slice 2 PR #7533 delivered the repeated-typecheck attribution receipt slice 1 required before any union implementation. decision_ratio=0 at every measured cell at N≤50 — priced benefit absent. Digest 3502539e920d9e40 derived by execution via node_criteria_digest on this tree after amending boundary/red_control/handback to record the verdict. The durable red control is shared_typecheck_distinct_compute_count_is_order_invariant; the one-shot ratio witnesses were deleted after the merged-SHA receipt, while the historical controls remain recorded in slice-2 receipt §C."

data namespace_binding_kernel_acceptance_note: String = "STATUS RECONCILIATION, NOT NEW WORK (operator ruling 2026-08-01). PR #7508 merged the supplied-candidate binding kernel, so the IMPLEMENTATION dependency for namespace-reference-derived-closure was resolved, but no acceptance receipt existed — leaving the roadmap saying its own prerequisite was unaccepted while the code depended on it. Two different truths for the same fact, which is exactly what node_is_startable reads, so P2a was being worked on while not honestly startable under the roadmap's own definition. The digest 2eba4715cdef42eb is the POST-#7530 value: #7530 rewrote this node's boundary, red_control and out_of_scope to cut authority from retirement, and roadmap_criteria_digest covers brief plus red_control plus handback, so the pre-amendment digest is stale by construction. It was DERIVED BY EXECUTION rather than transcribed — node_criteria_digest was run against the live node on this tree, not copied from a prior receipt or computed by hand. The red control names the malformed-transport refusal because that is the first arm #7530's amended row requires; the sibling arms (absent versus wrong-role identity distinct, duplicate supplied identity refused, wrong-category excluded, zero/one/two giving unbound/bound/ambiguous, exact declaration identity surviving, canonical fold invoked once) all execute in the same witness module and were green on the exact-head run that merged #7508."

data unreachable_patterns_construction_residue_note: String = "CONSTRUCTION RESIDUE declared by merged #6777 (emit: deny unreachable_patterns). Authority: src/v1/stage0_crates.dag unreachable_patterns_deny_note — validation landed; construction fix (group arms sharing an outer constructor into a nested match in freemonoid_match_arm_for) remains follow-on emitter work per that note, not landed on main. Pre-existing; NOT namespace scope; runs independently of N3-B waves.\n\nREPRODUCER (live on main): v1.gunbc.occurrence_binding_parser_walk declaration_exposure_from_containment — containment.ancestors match with depth-two Cons nesting: Empty => ModuleExposure; Cons { tail: Empty } => ModuleExposure; Cons { tail: Cons { head: _, tail: _ } } => LexicalExposure via occurrence_containment_parent_scope or ModuleExposure fallback.\n\nLOCALIZATION: v1_gunbc_occurrence_binding_parser_walk occurrence_containment_parent_scope in the same generated file lowers correctly (empty ancestors -> None, else -> Some(...)), so this is not a blanket FreeMonoid lowering failure: single-arm-per-outer-constructor lowering is correct in the one observed case. The defect is specific to multiple match arms sharing an outer constructor: freemonoid_match_arm_for selects arms |> filter(variant match) |> first — a second arm sharing the outer variant label but carrying a different discriminator body is silently dropped.\n\nB0 WORKAROUND (substrate not on main): N3-B0 exposure-authority adds std.occurrence_binding_candidates declaration_exposure_namespace_structural_root — an emit-safe depth-one parent-scope rewrite replacing a depth-two nested FreeMonoid match the emitter would collapse; deliberate scaffold, not a fix.\n\nDISSOLUTION TRIGGER — That B0 rewrite exists only because freemonoid_match_arm_for drops sibling arms sharing an outer constructor. Once the follow-on construction fix lands and generated output re-verifies, the rewrite may return to the direct nested match. Re-verify against generated output, not source, before dissolving.\n\nGENERATED DISCREPANCY (live on main): v1_gunbc_occurrence_binding_parser_walk declaration_exposure_from_containment — both the __fm.is_empty() branch and the else branch emit identical ModuleExposure { module }, where the source distinguishes empty-ancestor ModuleExposure from nested ModuleExposure/LexicalExposure. Expected after construction fix: three distinct outcomes (empty ancestors -> ModuleExposure; single ancestor -> ModuleExposure; nested ancestors -> LexicalExposure via occurrence_containment_parent_scope, ModuleExposure fallback when parent scope is absent)."

data namespace_cross_file_provenance_lane_integration_note: String = "N3-B STACKED LANE INTEGRATION (operator ruling 2026-08-07). See unreachable_patterns_construction_residue_note for the #6777 emitter construction residue tracked independently of this lane.\n\nPROGRESS STANDARD: namespace lane progress means A NAMED TERMINAL WITNESS WENT RED TO GREEN. Substrate PRs and repair PRs are necessary but are NOT namespace progress. B0 exposure/assembly and B1 fixture-proven projection carriers do not close namespace-cross-file-provenance; only the bound closing witness greens when OrdinaryLoadedCompilationClosure production lands (B2).\n\nCOMPLETION BOUNDARY: B0 lands one exposure authority and collision-free cross-file assembly substrate. B1 lands the complete clause-(e) projection carrier and discriminating fixture algebra; the N3-B row stays red because the ordinary loaded compilation producer is still absent. B2 lands that producer and greens the bound closing witness. #7770 remains quarry-only — manual extraction source, never a merge target; it closes unmerged once B0/B1/B2 land their extracted content.\n\nLANE SHAPE: B0, then B1; B2 and N3-C are parallel after B1. N3-D aggregate handback follows A-close plus B2 plus N3-C. Mutable fork bases, review state, and session topology live in the dashboard, not here."

data namespace_pool_independence_lane_brief_note: String = "N3-C (namespace-pool-independence) LANE BRIEF — operator ruling 2026-08-07. Undispatched until B1 lands; starts from the cleaned final B1 head, not from B0 and not from an intermediate B1 draft.\n\nCONTRACT: Compare two complete B1 clause-(e) projection results for the same source subject — baseline projection versus perturbed projection with one unrelated loaded file added. Required equality across both: same source subject; same reference occurrence identities; same selected declaration identities; same bound-reference population; same occurrence-level provenance rows; same file dependencies; same exact refusal outcome when applicable.\n\nLOAD-BEARING RED CONTROL: A perturbation that reselects a different declaration inside the same provider file while leaving the file-dependency set unchanged must be REFUSED by the full-carrier comparison. A dependency-set-only comparison passes this case while missing the exact Class B reselection N3-C exists to forbid — the comparison is over the complete ReferenceDerivedDependencyProjection carrier, not the deduplicated file list alone.\n\nDEPENDENCIES: Requires B1 (ReferenceDerivedDependencyProjection plus occurrence-grain provenance). Does not require B2 (ordinary loaded compilation closure producer)."

data false_verification_incident_mapping_note: String = "FIVE WAYS THE BUILD REPORTED A RESULT NOBODY EARNED, mapped to their exact homes rather than gathered under an umbrella node (consolidation ruling, 2026-08-08 — the parallel authority branch proposed one observation-verification-integrity node covering all five; a coarse identity over mechanisms this graph already decomposes would be the §3 second authority, so the incident record ports and the node does not). The mapping, one row each: a remote build that exits zero while its output never comes back is compute-artifact-return-and-materialization's first refusal; a stale local binary compared against itself is the same row's manifest-content refusal plus ci-remote-build-containment's stale-artefact refusal; a re-run that replays a combination computed when the change was opened is ci-owned-execution's fresh-combination control; no checks at all treated as satisfied, and a cancelled job rendered as a generic failure, are both ci-check-projection's typed endings — unobserved and cancelled each a distinct answer, never absorbed into pass or fail. Every future incident of this family files against exactly one of those homes; a sixth mechanism with no home is a finding about the graph, not a reason to mint the umbrella."

data namespace_closure_prerequisite_cut_note: String = "THE SIX CAPABILITIES INSIDE namespace-reference-derived-closure ARE NOT SIX NODES, and this cut adds exactly three rows in front of it rather than decomposing it (2026-08-04). Its closing contract, namespace_reference_derived_closure_contract, is a set-difference frontier over six capabilities under three triggers, and every row read Unavailable while the node itself was the only dispatchable thing in the lane. That is a single startable row standing for three separable pieces of work with different substrates, which is the coarse-node hazard namespace_node_recut_note already priced once.\n\nTHE CUT FOLLOWS THE TRIGGERS, because the triggers already record which work each capability waits on. namespace-structural-observations owns the four capabilities under P2aStructuralCandidateProducer7515 — same-parse-tree rules, whose substrate is the merged occurrence-binding parser walk. namespace-cross-file-provenance owns the one under P2aReferenceDependencyProjection7515. namespace-pool-independence owns the one under P2aPoolIndependentDependencyProjection7515.\n\nTHE FIRST TWO ARE PARALLEL, NOT SEQUENTIAL. No dependency runs from the same-file rules to the cross-file projection; they share only the contract they feed. Sequencing them would have idled one worker for no modeled reason. Pool independence DOES depend on cross-file provenance, because a differential needs a projector to perturb.\n\nWHY THE CROSS-FILE ROW IS THE LARGE ONE. The merged parser walk resolves nothing across a file boundary — its own authority note scopes it to authored source through parse_with_table, with transport and module path from the parser. So cross-file target realization sits INSIDE that row rather than upstream of it, and the trigger being named after a merged PR does not mean the capability is discharged.\n\nNO NEW IDENTITY WAS MINTED FOR THE INTEGRATION OR FOR THE CENSUS. namespace-reference-derived-closure keeps its durable identity and now denotes the aggregate handback — all six observations admitted, the expecting-red enrolment retired, its existing check green — so its first_slice moved to say so while its boundary, which still covers all six, did not. namespace-ambiguity-discharge likewise keeps its identity and its existing dependency on the closure node; the census it owns is authoritative only once all six close, never after the same-file rows alone, because a census taken before cross-file answers exist undercounts exactly the candidates that cross a file boundary and inherits the pool coincidence pool-independence exists to remove.\n\nTHE THREE NEW ROWS ARE DELIBERATELY UNBOUND. Each first_slice names authoring its own closing check as its first act, per v1_lane_binding_survey_note: the contract follows the witness, never precedes it. Authoring contracts here to make the rows look startable is the coverage-by-illusion tier that note forbids, and pool-independence could not honestly carry one at all — its differential compares an output shape its own prerequisite has not yet produced."

data scm_prerequisite_acceptance_note: String = "The operator explicitly accepted the three independent R0 models and the separate P−1 evidence carrier, then accepted R1 after merging its three-realization compatibility extraction, and accepted P0 after its layered landing spine and twenty-two executing journey witnesses landed. R1 depends only on the three R0 receipts; P0 consumes both accepted R1 and accepted P−1. These receipts preserve every prerequisite and handback as durable facts. Replacing accepted rows with their child would erase history and violate roadmap_acceptance_receipt_note."

data scm_p0_acceptance_evidence_note: String = "P0 acceptance maps the roadmap clauses through test.claim.source_integration_landing_spine_witness witness_p0_roadmap_acceptance_contract_holds: all seven removed closure receipts stay machine-owned; evidence support/challenge/neither/conflict and terminal blocking do not become a preference question; both material alternatives resume by structural position; authorization, self-amendment, exhausted-bound, and permanent-effect failures keep their typed internal refusal and plain terminal projection; stale-parent work retries internally; a committed independently read-back transition stays Landed through projection failure; the audience projection excludes internal theory and manual Git-repair vocabulary; and the successful path retains its ordinary Git compatibility receipt. The same aggregate executes the explicit 50-agent/10-overlap/30-minute stress profile and timestamp non-authority. Handback remains bounded exactly at P0: gunbc.source_integration_landing_spine names the absent P1 proof kernel, P2 capture/edit lens, and P3 effect transport; none is claimed or activated by this receipt."

data v1_interpreter_primitive_roster_acceptance_note: String = "ACCEPTED AT THE NARROWED BAR, WITH ONE HANDBACK CLAUSE GENUINELY AMENDED RATHER THAN REWORDED -- read the digest move as a real change to the bar, which is exactly the check criteria_digest_reword_note asks a reviewer to perform. The clause as #7558 landed it demanded 'witnesses asserting exact values rather than lower bounds'. Those witnesses no longer exist on main: #7615 deleted the five that pinned live-population counts (7 dispatch sites, 183 derived rows, 2 declared rows, 174 arm identities, 161 authored spellings) because DESIGN.md 5 rules that a measurement copied from the same current tree is not an oracle -- and those five had already behaved exactly as that rule predicts, as a change detector that redded main twice (#7575 landed one derived arm short, #7614 repaired, #7615 removed the class). Accepting against the original wording would have required either restoring a forbidden oracle or calling a clause satisfied that plainly was not.\n\nWHAT REPLACED IT IS STRONGER, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS NOT A LOWERED BAR. The deleted clause existed so that a silently dropped arm stays detectable, which a lower-bound witness cannot do. Every exact assertion grounded in a NAMED identity or a controlled fixture survived #7615 untouched: free_call_shadowing_is_exactly_the_two_inert_lens_bridges and method_call_shadowing_is_exactly_the_known_lookup_case assert exact shadow sets by member name (CITATION CORRECTION, 2026-08-11: the first of those no longer exists under that name. The inert-lens census, its two host builtins and the v2.lens.inert_lens module were deleted whole, so the free-call shadow set emptied and the witness became free_call_carries_no_shadowed_spelling, an exact set assertion over an empty set rather than over two named members. That is a genuinely weaker witness than the one this note cites as surviving evidence -- an empty-set claim cannot discriminate a detector that never reports -- and the discrimination is carried instead by the planted-duplicate controls and by method_call_shadowing_is_exactly_the_known_lookup_case, which still pins a named member. Recorded here rather than silently reworded, because the sentence was cited as evidence and the evidence changed); the_two_shadowed_arm_identities_under_lookup_are_exactly_the_expected_pair pins both identities; the_declared_residue_is_exactly_the_two_non_match_arm_sites is a two-directional set equality by name rather than a count. Only the tree-copied census literals went, which is precisely the line DESIGN.md 5 draws when it says the rule rejects tree-copied census pins while preserving exact-count fixture tests. The three denominators were NOT deleted: dispatch_site_count, distinct_arm_identity_count and authored_spelling_count all remain derived from the roster rather than pinned as literals, which is what this node's handback asks for. AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS NOTE OVERCLAIMED WHAT CONSUMES THEM, and the correction is recorded here rather than quietly edited because it is the same failure this repository keeps paying for -- a sentence that reads well while the evidence it cites does not establish it (caught in review 46995). That draft said all three 'gained a live consumer at D1' which 'joins census to roster by identity'. Neither half was true. What test.claim.primitive_identity_join_witness w_interpreter_census_consumes_roster_authority actually executes is distinct_arm_identity_count, authored_spelling_count and v1_interpreter_row_count: it asserts the ordering relation the deleted witness used to pin, plus primitive_d0_interpreter_census_matches_roster, which is primitive_d0_interpreter_surface_row_count() == v1_interpreter_row_count() -- a COUNT EQUALITY between two independent derivations of the same population, not an identity join. That is a legitimate cross-derivation reconciliation and it is not a tree-copied census literal, but DESIGN.md 5 is explicit that completeness is an identity join rather than a count equality, so it must not be described as one. And dispatch_site_count has NO external consumer at all: the only mention outside its own carrier is this note. That is named here as residue rather than counted as delivered. DISSOLVE-ON: the D0 identity join reaching arm grain, at which point the census and the roster reconcile by row identity and the count equality is replaced rather than supplemented.\n\nEVIDENCE, DERIVED BY EXECUTION. Digest 581758f45921b40a was produced by running node_criteria_digest against the live amended node on this tree, not transcribed or hand-computed; the discriminating control for the pin itself is that two earlier wordings of the same row yield different values -- d2e455ab49e472e2 before any amendment, f0f450f4ddc82fe9 before the review-46995 correction to the denominator clause -- so the stored digest demonstrably tracks the criteria text rather than merely existing, and each correction to the bar has been re-derived rather than carried forward. All 19 witnesses in test.claim.v1_interpreter_primitive_surface_witness_test executed green on a binary REBUILT AT THIS HEAD, which matters more here than in most acceptances: the roster derives from macro token lists compiled INTO the binary, so a stale target answers about a tree that no longer exists, and that is exactly how #7575's shortfall stayed invisible to local runs. The named red_control is shadow_detector_catches_a_planted_duplicate, the planted-defect RED discharging this row's second red_control arm, paired with shadow_detector_admits_a_real_alias_pair so that a detector refusing legitimate aliases would fail too.\n\nWHAT THIS RECEIPT DOES NOT CLAIM. The first red_control arm -- an arm without a row and a row without an arm are both unwritable -- is discharged BY CONSTRUCTION, one macro token list expanding both, and therefore has no executing witness by design; the node's own first_slice already states it is mechanically preventive for the sites it covers and explicitly not structural, because a whole new dispatch site can still be added in Rust unnoticed. Closing that is v1-interpreter-primitive-dispatch-authority, which is NOT implemented and NOT accepted here -- and the record needs one correction, because a rollup briefly read it as landed: PR #7624 carried the title 'Lane B: interpreter roster R1 -- invert dispatch authority from Rust to the .dag roster', but its entire merged diff is two lines adding `import std.types { NonEmptyStr }` to this carrier. It inverted no authority, generated no dispatch, and deleted no Rust-side denominator; it is a harmless missing-import repair whose title described work that was never written (operator correction, 2026-08-02). R1 is unstarted, so accepting R0 here advances the frontier to an unimplemented successor rather than past it. The third arm -- a roster able to represent an arm carrying no semantic primitive identity -- holds STRUCTURALLY, because InterpreterPrimitiveDispatchArm carries an interpreter-local arm identity and no semantic-primitive-identity field at all, so every row already is such an arm and the closing contract shows the roster's operations are total over one. An earlier cut of that clause discharged it with D1's primitive_d0_derived_rows_missing_identity_count() > 0, which measures how far the semantic identity join has got rather than what the roster can carry, and would have gone red exactly when the join succeeded for every derived row -- legitimate downstream progress reddening an upstream node's acceptance check, the same defect class #7615 removed from the census witnesses relocated onto join state (caught in review 46974, corrected before merge). The seed-file reconciliation clause is delivered as a construction plus a named residue (every primitive arm now carries the token form arm \"identity\" { \"spelling\" } =>, so the residue is whatever stays bare) and is NOT asserted by an executing witness; that is a real limit of this handback, stated rather than implied."

type RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjection
  = RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjected { receipts: List<RoadmapAcceptanceReceipt> }
  | RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjectionRefused { detail: String }

fn roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load() -> RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoad {
  load_roadmap_acceptance_event_history(path: roadmap_acceptance_event_history_carrier_repo_path)
}

data roadmap_acceptance_history_note: String = "roadmap_acceptance_event_history is the single authority for acceptance facts, loaded from dag/gunbc/roadmap_acceptance_event_history.jsonl (append-only JSONL carrier). roadmap_acceptance_receipts projects the live receipt set by replaying recorded and revoked events only — never a second editable receipt list; LoadRefused is a typed RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjectionRefused, never an empty receipt list. New acceptance appends AcceptanceRecorded to the carrier; withdrawal appends AcceptanceRevoked naming the exact prior receipt, a disposition, and audit fields. Authored seal count/digest are deleted: prefix law is enforced by gunbc.roadmap_acceptance_history_observation parsing merge-base carrier content via git.Core.Show — a changed carrier without valid merge-base parse refuses AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityRefusedPriorHistoryObservation, never prior_history = []."

fn roadmap_acceptance_receipts_from_carrier_text(text: String) -> RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjection {
  match load_roadmap_acceptance_event_history_from_carrier_text(text: text) {
    RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoaded { events: events } =>
      RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjected {
        receipts: project_live_acceptance_receipts(history: events),
      }
    RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoadRefused { detail: detail } =>
      RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
  }
}

fn roadmap_acceptance_receipts() -> RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjection {
  match roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load() {
    RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoaded { events: events } =>
      RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjected {
        receipts: project_live_acceptance_receipts(history: events),
      }
    RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoadRefused { detail: detail } =>
      RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
  }
}

fn roadmap_acceptance_history_integrity_verdict_on_authority() -> AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityVerdict {
  match roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load() {
    RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoadRefused { detail: detail } =>
      AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityRefusedPriorHistoryObservation {
        detail: concat("current history load refused: ", detail),
      }
    RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoaded { events: current } =>
      acceptance_history_integrity_verdict_from_prior_observation(
        observation: current_roadmap_acceptance_history_prior_observation(current_history: current),
        history: current,
      )
  }
}

fn roadmap_acceptance_history_integrity_holds() -> Bool {
  match roadmap_acceptance_history_integrity_verdict_on_authority() {
    AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityHolds => true
    AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityRefusedReplay => false
    AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityRefusedVanishedWithoutRevocation { receipt: _ } => false
    AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityRefusedPriorPrefix { index: _ } => false
    AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityRefusedPriorHistoryObservation { detail: _ } => false
  }
}

data v2_emitter_producer_provenance_acceptance_note: String = "ACCEPTANCE OF LANDED WORK, the exact state closing_contract_task_naming_correction_note names this node as the specimen of: bound, implemented, green, awaiting the operator act. The landing is the Gate-A oracle (#7408, operator-merged): SelfEmitted requires an executed probe receipt, so an emitter-produced claim without a receipt from the run that made it is unrepresentable — the ticket's own red control. Evidence, executed 2026-08-02 on the tree carrying this receipt: all 27 witness fns in the contract's entry file pass, including the bound closing validation (a restored binding without an executed receipt does not authorize) and its sibling refusal controls (hand-forged authorization, minted executed verdicts, synthesized digests, fabricated build-greens all red). The digest f1c76111c83a0a60 was DERIVED BY EXECUTION — node_criteria_digest run against the live node on this tree, not transcribed. The handback's three elements are on main: the relabelled roster (every frontier row producer-qualified), the retired single count (per-producer baselines, witness_producer_qualified_baselines_holds), and the refusal demonstration (the _reds suite). Acceptance is the operator's merge of the change adding this receipt; the receipt is authored by the roadmap steward as the vehicle, never as the act."

data v1_seed_honesty_decision_acceptance_note: String = "THE NODE'S HANDBACK WAS TWO THINGS -- a check that can fail, plus the decision recorded either way -- and only the first half existed before this change. The decision procedure landed earlier and is genuinely discriminating: a reflexive claim/check refuses with bootstrap_self_verify_same_artifact, a planted divergence with bootstrap_self_verify_diverged, mismatched provenance with bootstrap_self_verify_provenance_mismatch, and a distinct equal-content pair with equal provenance ADMITS, so none of the three refusals is a blanket red. seed_honesty_decision was SeedHonestyUndecided, so the aggregate stayed red and was enrolled QuarantineProbeExpectRed rather than reported done over the missing half. This change records the operator verdict of 2026-08-02, the aggregate greens, and the quarantine enrollment is deleted in the same commit. Digest 1717cfe258d9afe1 was DERIVED BY EXECUTION -- node_criteria_digest run against the live node on this tree -- and the probe carried its own control: it reproduced f1c76111c83a0a60 for v2-emitter-producer-provenance, byte-matching the receipt an unrelated change had already stored for that node, and returned three distinct values for three nodes, so it is computing the criteria rather than answering with a constant.\n\nTHE VERDICT IS FixedPointTrustSufficient, AND THE NAME UNDERSELLS ITS CONDITIONS. It does not say a fixed point is trustworthy. It says v1 deletion does not require an independently implemented second compiler, and requires instead an explicit generation-admission pipeline: each stage executes against a declared requirement revision, binds its exact inputs, outputs, producer and verdict, and prevents downstream use when its local requirements fail; a candidate generation may be produced and inspected but cannot promote itself; promotion requires declared requirement changes, complete stage and behavioral receipts, no undeclared v1 fallback, and a retained known-good recovery path; breaking requirement changes require an expand-migrate-contract bridge; and the previous generation stays available until the promoted compiler demonstrates it can produce a valid CHANGED successor rather than merely reproduce itself. That program is designed in docs/plans/emission-admission-stage-aware-pipeline-design.md (gunbc#7681, in review at the time of writing, itself carrying operator-requested changes) and is UNBUILT; no roadmap node carries it yet. This receipt accepts the decision, not the pipeline the decision requires.\n\nTHREE THINGS THE VERDICT EXPLICITLY DOES NOT PROTECT, carried on the rationale itself so a later reader cannot infer them from the node being green. A fixed point does not defeat a self-reproducing defect -- stage1 equalling stage2 is exactly what such a defect also produces, and that residual is ACCEPTED here rather than ruled out. seed_self_verify_check is a proven decision procedure and NOT the live trust-path authority: the realized-comparison and regen paths do not route their artifact identities through it, this change does not wire that path, and the generation-admission program owns it. Rollback and fix-forward are different guarantees -- committed emitted Rust gives rollback to any earlier generation but does not by itself give fix-forward once a requirement revision has changed what the previous generation can read, and the bootstrap reachability invariant that addresses this is unbuilt today. An earlier draft of the rationale argued DDC was unnecessary because the seed's source is readable. That argument is WRONG and was corrected before this receipt: the classic attack is a binary miscompiling readable source, so readability is not the defense. What the rationale now claims is narrower and is a mitigation rather than a proof -- the emitted Rust is committed as plain text and re-compared by the regen gate, so a perpetuated defect must appear in a readable diff AND survive review of it.\n\nTHE RED CONTROL IS A FLIPPED EXPECT-RED PROBE, NOT A NEW TEST. seed_honesty_undecided_keeps_closing_contract_red read the live decision constant, so the verdict landing left it able to assert only a state that no longer exists -- it had to go red or be deleted, and DESIGN 4b(4) forbids deleting the evidence with the machinery. The conjunction is now a function of the decision applied to the live constant by the contract and to SeedHonestyUndecided by the permanent control. That control is what keeps the fifth conjunct load-bearing: weaken the decision arm so an undecided value satisfies the contract and the contract stays green while only the control reds."

type AcceptedRoadmapNodesProjection
  = AcceptedRoadmapNodesProjected { nodes: List<RoadmapNode> }
  | AcceptedRoadmapNodesProjectionRefused { detail: String }

type AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjection
  = AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjected { ids: List<RoadmapNodeId> }
  | AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjectionRefused { detail: String }

type RoadmapAuthorityProjection
  = RoadmapAuthorityProjected { doc: RoadmapDocument, accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId> }
  | RoadmapAuthorityProjectionRefused { detail: String }

fn accepted_roadmap_nodes_projection() -> AcceptedRoadmapNodesProjection {
  match roadmap_acceptance_receipts() {
    RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjected { receipts: receipts } =>
      AcceptedRoadmapNodesProjected {
        nodes: declared_roadmap_nodes() |> filter(n => node_accepted(receipts: receipts, rn: n)),
      }
    RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      AcceptedRoadmapNodesProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
  }
}

fn accepted_roadmap_node_ids_projection_from_carrier_text(text: String) -> AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjection {
  match roadmap_acceptance_receipts_from_carrier_text(text: text) {
    RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjected { receipts: receipts } =>
      AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjected {
        ids: accepted_node_ids_from_receipts(receipts: receipts),
      }
    RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
  }
}

fn accepted_roadmap_node_ids_projection() -> AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjection {
  match accepted_roadmap_nodes_projection() {
    AcceptedRoadmapNodesProjected { nodes: nodes } =>
      AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjected { ids: nodes |> map(n => n.node) }
    AcceptedRoadmapNodesProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
  }
}

fn active_roadmap_nodes_for_accepted(accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>) -> List<RoadmapNode> {
  declared_roadmap_nodes() |> filter(n => !(accepted |> any(a => a == n.node)))
}

type ActiveRoadmapNodesProjection
  = ActiveRoadmapNodesProjected { nodes: List<RoadmapNode> }
  | ActiveRoadmapNodesProjectionRefused { detail: String }

type ActiveRoadmapEdgesProjection
  = ActiveRoadmapEdgesProjected { edges: List<RoadmapEdge> }
  | ActiveRoadmapEdgesProjectionRefused { detail: String }

fn active_roadmap_nodes_projection() -> ActiveRoadmapNodesProjection {
  match accepted_roadmap_node_ids_projection() {
    AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjected { ids: accepted } =>
      ActiveRoadmapNodesProjected {
        nodes: active_roadmap_nodes_for_accepted(accepted: accepted),
      }
    AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      ActiveRoadmapNodesProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
  }
}

fn active_roadmap_edges_projection() -> ActiveRoadmapEdgesProjection {
  match accepted_roadmap_node_ids_projection() {
    AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjected { ids: accepted } =>
      ActiveRoadmapEdgesProjected {
        edges: active_roadmap_edges_for_accepted(accepted: accepted),
      }
    AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      ActiveRoadmapEdgesProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
  }
}

data closing_contract_task_projection_boundary_note: String = "The derived tasks are added at the PROJECTION, not to active_roadmap_nodes_for_accepted. Folding them into the active set reded witness_active_set_is_declared_minus_accepted and witness_accepted_parent_discharges_active_dependency, and those witnesses are right: 'active = declared minus accepted' is a law about the AUTHORITY, and a derived projection row is not a declared deliverable. Keeping the law intact is also what makes the derivation safe — the authority stays exactly what someone authored, and the frontier view is a function of it."

data closing_contract_task_naming_correction_note: String = "THESE ARE CLOSING-CONTRACT TASKS, NOT ACCEPTANCE TASKS, and the earlier name caused a real category error. The projection computes active AND ExecutionContractUnspecified AND every-prerequisite-accepted, which is the set of nodes whose completion nothing can yet CHECK. Authoring that check is contract work. Acceptance is a separate, later, operator act on work already done and verified.\n\nThe old name made those two look like one state, and a node can be in any of several: blocked on unaccepted prerequisites; missing a closing contract; dispatchable; implemented but verification-red; implemented and green, awaiting operator acceptance; accepted. v2-emitter-producer-provenance is the specimen that exposed it — it is BOUND, so it never appears in this projection, while what it actually needs is acceptance of landed work. A projection named for acceptance that structurally cannot surface the thing needing acceptance is misnamed, so it was renamed to startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract_for_accepted.\n\nKNOWN GAP, recorded rather than papered over: a derived task is itself built with ExecutionContractUnspecified, and dispatch refuses an unspecified contract. So the belt cannot dispatch these rows — the target waits on a contract, and the contract-authoring row has no contract of its own. Manual child spawning works around it; end-to-end dispatch does not. Closing it wants ONE parameterized ClosingContractAuthoring kind whose completion predicate is reusable across every target (target now carries exactly one contract, with at least one validation, every referenced validation resolves, a planted failure shows the validation can reject, and the contract names the same target identity) rather than ten bespoke meta-witnesses. Not landed here; this note is its trigger."

data closing_contract_task_derivation_note: String = "CLOSING-CONTRACT TASKS ARE DERIVED, NOT AUTHORED (operator ruling 2026-07-29: 'you shouldn't need to manually maintain that stuff — the relationship should be inferred from one side or the other'). The first cut hand-wrote four acceptance nodes and four edges, and one had to be hand-removed within minutes because pderive-typesafe-nullary-reflection was already accepted: a new prerequisite on completed work retroactively un-satisfies it, which witness_accepted_parents_precede_dependents caught. That is the hand-maintained-count failure this repository refuses everywhere else — two things that must be kept in step, drifting the moment either side moves.\n\nThe relationship is a FUNCTION of the node: startable (every prerequisite accepted) AND unbound (ExecutionContractUnspecified) means its closing validation does not exist, so authoring that validation is the work that unblocks it. Nothing else needs saying. An accepted node leaves the active set and its task disappears; a node that gains a contract stops being unbound and its task disappears; a node whose prerequisite lands becomes startable and its task appears. No cleanup step exists because there is nothing to clean.\n\nThe frontier is deliberately STARTABLE-only rather than every unbound node. A terminus like v1-zero-hand-maintained-rust is unbound too, but surfacing a closing-contract task for work whose prerequisites are years out is noise that would bury the four rows that matter today.\n\nOn identity: a derived row takes a deterministic id from its target rather than a minted one, and that is consistent with the identity law rather than an exception to it. The law governs declared DELIVERABLES, which must survive rename and reordering. A closing-contract task is a projection of a frontier state — it has no history to preserve, it exists exactly while its target lacks a contract, and if the target's slug changes it is honestly a different task."

fn node_is_startable_for_accepted(n: RoadmapNode, accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>) -> Bool {
  all(
    roadmap_parent_ids(edges: declared_roadmap_edges(), node: n.node),
    p => accepted |> any(a => a == (p as String)),
  )
}

data node_closing_validation_defork_note: String = "This used to answer the binding question locally, and its answer differed from the verifier's: any ExecutionContract counted as bound here, while an empty validation list counted as UNBOUND in gunbc.roadmap_verify and refused in gunbc.roadmap_dispatch_environment. It now reads the single authority, so a node bound to a contract that closes nothing keeps its acceptance task instead of vanishing from the frontier while remaining unverifiable."

fn node_lacks_closing_validation(n: RoadmapNode) -> Bool {
  !contract_has_closing_validation(contract: n.execution)
}

fn ticket_headline_of(n: RoadmapNode) -> String {
  match line_ticket(item: n.line) {
    Present { value: t } => t.headline
    Absent => n.node as String
  }
}

fn ticket_red_control_of(n: RoadmapNode) -> String {
  match line_ticket(item: n.line) {
    Present { value: t } => t.red_control
    Absent => "the node states no red control"
  }
}

data roadmap_identity_prefix_is_cosmetic_note: String = "DO NOT 'FIX' 2-scm-compatibility-shape's rnfx_ IDENTITY PREFIX. It reads as a test-fixture prefix where every other declared node uses rn_, and that asymmetry is real but it is COSMETIC: no witness asserts any prefix, and roadmap_node_identity_law constrains what may not be an identity INPUT (slug, title, criteria digest, containment position, dependency neighbourhood, declaration order, file position), never the alphabet of the minted value.\n\nRewriting it would be the actual violation. The law's whole persistence argument is that an identity must survive edits, reordering, regeneration and independent branches; mutating a live identity for tidiness is precisely the churn it exists to forbid, and it would silently break anything holding the old value. The prefix carries no meaning to any mechanism, so the honest disposition is to leave it and record why — which is what this row is for. A prefix convention worth enforcing would be enforced at MINT time by construction, not by editing identities already in the graph."

fn startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract_for_accepted(accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>) -> List<RoadmapNode> {
  declared_roadmap_nodes()
    |> filter(n => !(accepted |> any(a => a == n.node)))
    |> filter(n => node_lacks_closing_validation(n: n))
    |> filter(n => node_is_startable_for_accepted(n: n, accepted: accepted))
}

fn closing_contract_task_for(n: RoadmapNode) -> RoadmapNode {
  RoadmapNode {
    identity: concat(n.identity as String, "-closing-contract") as RoadmapNodeIdentity,
    node: concat(n.node as String, "-closing-contract") as RoadmapNodeId,
    line: TicketLine {
      done: false,
      ticket: fields(
        headline: concat("Write the closing check for: ", ticket_headline_of(n: n)),
        boundary: concat("One executable check states when this is done, transcribed from the node's own bar: ", ticket_red_control_of(n: n)),
        displaced_cost: "Turns a prose bar into a runnable verdict, so the node it gates can be dispatched and its completion checked rather than judged.",
        first_slice: "Write the check and prove each refusal arm fires on a planted input.",
        red_control: concat("The check must fail when the condition fails: ", ticket_red_control_of(n: n)),
        out_of_scope: "Write the statement of done; do not do the work it gates.",
        handback: "Return the executable check, each arm firing on a planted input, and the gated node bound to it."
      ),
    },
    carriers: n.carriers,
    sizing: Sized {
      intricacy: IntricacyLow,
      volume: VolumeSmall,
      repo: "gunbc",
      acceptance: ManualAcceptance,
    },
    execution: ExecutionContractUnspecified,
    owner: n.owner,
    signoff: Unsigned,
  }
}

data closing_contract_family_claim_note: String = "The family claim, stated once (presentation family-once grouping): every closing-contract task shares this claim, and each generated ticket's fused headline ('Write the closing check for: X') repeats it per row. The pair surface below exposes the DISCRIMINATING SUBJECT (the gated node's own typed ticket headline) beside each generated task so a projection can state the claim once and never recover the subject by parsing the fused headline. The generated ticket itself is unchanged — ROADMAP.md rendering and dispatch briefs keep the full self-contained headline."

data closing_contract_family_claim: String = "Write the closing check — one executable done-condition for each gated node below"

type ClosingContractTaskPair {
  task: RoadmapNode
  subject: String
}

type ClosingContractTaskPairsProjection
  = ClosingContractTaskPairsProjected { pairs: List<ClosingContractTaskPair> }
  | ClosingContractTaskPairsProjectionRefused { detail: String }

type ClosingContractTaskEdgesProjection
  = ClosingContractTaskEdgesProjected { edges: List<RoadmapEdge> }
  | ClosingContractTaskEdgesProjectionRefused { detail: String }

fn closing_contract_task_pairs_for_accepted(accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>) -> List<ClosingContractTaskPair> {
  startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract_for_accepted(accepted: accepted)
    |> map(n => ClosingContractTaskPair {
      task: closing_contract_task_for(n: n),
      subject: ticket_headline_of(n: n),
    })
}

fn closing_contract_task_pairs_projection() -> ClosingContractTaskPairsProjection {
  match accepted_roadmap_node_ids_projection() {
    AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      ClosingContractTaskPairsProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
    AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjected { ids: accepted } =>
      ClosingContractTaskPairsProjected {
        pairs: closing_contract_task_pairs_for_accepted(accepted: accepted),
      }
  }
}

fn closing_contract_task_edges_projection() -> ClosingContractTaskEdgesProjection {
  match accepted_roadmap_node_ids_projection() {
    AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      ClosingContractTaskEdgesProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
    AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjected { ids: accepted } =>
      ClosingContractTaskEdgesProjected {
        edges: closing_contract_task_edges_for_accepted(accepted: accepted),
      }
  }
}

type ProjectedRoadmapDependencyEdgesProjection
  = ProjectedRoadmapDependencyEdgesProjected { edges: List<RoadmapEdge> }
  | ProjectedRoadmapDependencyEdgesProjectionRefused { detail: String }

data projected_roadmap_dependency_edges_note: String = "Machine-consumed dependency edges for dispatch, program view, and frontier startability: declared active edges plus derived closing-contract task edges. Consumers must read this projection rather than declared_roadmap_edges alone — hand-maintained declared edges omit closing-contract dependencies that the authority derives from acceptance state."

fn projected_roadmap_dependency_edges_projection() -> ProjectedRoadmapDependencyEdgesProjection {
  match accepted_roadmap_node_ids_projection() {
    AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      ProjectedRoadmapDependencyEdgesProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
    AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjected { ids: accepted } =>
      ProjectedRoadmapDependencyEdgesProjected {
        edges: projected_roadmap_edges_for_accepted(accepted: accepted),
      }
  }
}

fn closing_contract_task_edges_for_accepted(accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>) -> List<RoadmapEdge> {
  startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract_for_accepted(accepted: accepted)
    |> map(n => RoadmapEdge {
      child: n.node,
      parent: concat(n.node as String, "-closing-contract") as RoadmapNodeId,
    })
}

fn active_roadmap_edges_for_accepted(accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>) -> List<RoadmapEdge> {
  declared_roadmap_edges() |> filter(e =>
    !(accepted |> any(a => a == e.child)) && !(accepted |> any(a => a == e.parent)))
}

fn preamble() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    HeadingBlock { level: H1, inlines: [TextInline { text: "gunbc — Roadmap" }] },
    ParagraphBlock { inlines: [TextInline { text: "`DESIGN.md` is the authority for why. This file projects the remaining committed deliverables and their dependency order from `dag/gunbc/roadmap_authority.dag`. The authority declares every node and records a typed acceptance receipt per accepted node; the active set is derived as declared minus validly accepted, so acceptance never deletes a row and this projection stays active-only. It carries no mutable pull-request, CI, session, or attempt state. Implementation evidence does not equal acceptance; a merged but unaccepted deliverable remains active." }] },
    ParagraphBlock { inlines: [TextInline { text: "Indented rows depend on the row above them. Some deliverables have additional cross-chain prerequisites represented by `RoadmapEdge` in the authority. Every active row requires explicit manual acceptance. Automatic admission from this refreshed authority is future work; the existing manual dispatch path remains live and outside this model slice. Ready may schedule only after every dependency is accepted; it never overrides a dependency or refusal. Parked plans remain reachable through the documentation graph but are not active roadmap rows." }] },
    ParagraphBlock { inlines: [TextInline { text: "Parked context (non-dispatchable): [compiler algorithm survey](docs/plans/compiler-algorithm-survey-2026-07-04.md) · [dispatch maintain CC](docs/plans/dispatch-maintain-cc.md) · [eval bind proposal](docs/plans/eval-bind-node-eval-propose.md) · [interpreter memory chronicle](docs/plans/interpreter-memory-chronicle.md) · [node/subtree visibility grants](docs/plans/node-subtree-visibility-grants.md) · [non-fold residue catalogue](docs/plans/non-fold-irreducible-residue-catalogue.md) · [post-engine roadmap](docs/plans/post-engine-pr-roadmap.md) · [post-zero regen placement](docs/plans/post-zero-regen-gate-placement.md) · [syntactic sample audit](docs/plans/real-debt-syntactic-sample-audit.md) · [regen 31/32 reconciliation](docs/plans/regen-divergence-31-vs-32-reconciliation.md) · [regen divergence ratchet](docs/plans/regen-divergence-ratchet-shape.md) · [roadmap spawner](docs/plans/roadmap-spawner.md) · [seed honesty discharge](docs/plans/seed-honesty-discharge-design.md) · [space lens minimal project](docs/plans/space-lens-minimal-project.md)." }] },
  ]
}

data roadmap_focus_selection_note: String = "THE ONE ROW THAT SETS THE VIEW. Focusing is a projection policy, not an edit — every node and edge stays declared in this file, the dispatch surface still sees the whole graph, and setting this back to NoFocus restores the full page with no recovery step. The projection states how many it is hiding, derived rather than transcribed, so a focused page can never read as a shorter roadmap.\n\nMOVED TO THE INFRASTRUCTURE LANES (operator direction, 2026-08-07). It previously selected the v1 exit lane and the compiler-guarantee climbs. Those remain real work and are unchanged in this file; they are off the page because forward movement on them was stopped. The stated reason is that infrastructure buckled underneath them — a fleet whose applied state cannot be answered without logging into machines, a required path whose cost is dominated by building and testing, machine faults arriving as reds that look like code faults, and generated artefacts conflicting over lists nobody disagrees about. Work carried out on top of that produces results nobody can trust, so the page shows the lanes that repair it and the lane that makes a merge to main become applied fleet state.\n\nTHE JUDGMENT LANE IS DELIBERATELY OFF THE PAGE while its prerequisites are on it: the machines that would serve its model are converging in the fleet lane, and putting the consumer beside the prerequisite would invite starting it early. It is declared, not parked. THE DETERMINISTIC CONFIDENCE LANE IS ON THE PAGE and is not the judgment lane: repository inspection — what exists, who consumes it, what a change may affect — is immediate development infrastructure that must work with no LLM and no Spark provisioned, so it does not wait behind serving. The judgment service depends on it as its grounding packet; the dependency edge carries that order.\n\nA FOCUS IS A DISPLAY CHOICE, NEVER A DISPOSITION, and the difference matters more now than it did when this row selected two lanes. Off the page currently sits real retained work with real remaining boundaries — the namespace closure re-observed on an exact main subject at four of six capabilities established (A–D; the two dependency-projection capabilities remain unavailable under their P2a triggers, closing frontier two — 2026-08-08 execution receipt, superseding the earlier none-admitted wording), the v1 exit with no module yet crossed and no hand-written Rust yet drained, the shell production vertical, the staged alert program, the frozen TypeScript boundary, the instrument adoption, the deployment activation receipt. Hidden means selected against, and nothing here records WHY a hidden lane is paused or what restarts it. That is a missing dimension on the node, not a fact this projection policy can carry: a lane frozen behind infrastructure, a lane draining to merge, and a lane parked because its hypothesis was falsified are three different states, and today all three read identically as absent. Supplying it is a change to the node shape and to what the page counts, and it lands next; until it does, read a hidden lane as retained and unexplained rather than as decided."

data roadmap_focus_selection: RoadmapFocus = FocusOnOwners {
  label: "the compute fabric + the infrastructure stabilisation lanes + fleet convergence from main",
  owners: ["compute", "fleet", "ci-placement", "ci-control", "generated-artifact", "ci-cost", "confidence"],
}

fn projected_roadmap_nodes_for_accepted(accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>) -> List<RoadmapNode> {
  concat(
    active_roadmap_nodes_for_accepted(accepted: accepted),
    startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract_for_accepted(accepted: accepted)
      |> map(n => closing_contract_task_for(n: n)),
  )
}

fn projected_roadmap_edges_for_accepted(accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>) -> List<RoadmapEdge> {
  concat(
    active_roadmap_edges_for_accepted(accepted: accepted),
    closing_contract_task_edges_for_accepted(accepted: accepted),
  )
}

fn active_section_for_acceptance(focus: RoadmapFocus, accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>) -> RoadmapSection {
  let nodes = projected_roadmap_nodes_for_accepted(accepted: accepted)
  let edges = projected_roadmap_edges_for_accepted(accepted: accepted)
  RoadmapSection {
    title: "Active deliverables — dependency order",
    elements: concat(
      [
        section_prose(content: "The graph has sixteen lanes: SCM compatibility · namespace · P-derive · observation · placement · compute · CI cost · CI control · generated artefacts · v1 exit (four finish lines: compiler fixed point, interpreter deleted, products v1-free, zero hand-maintained Rust) · shell · roadmap runtime · fleet/hygiene · judgment · hermetic toolchain · compiler-guarantee (the DESIGN §4b ladder climbs; rung STATE lives in the guarantee claims carrier and is emitted, never restated in tickets — [gap analysis](docs/plans/compiler-guarantee-recovery-gap-analysis.md)). The three independent SCM R0 models, the separate P−1 evidence carrier, R1 compatibility-shape extraction, and P0 user-contract/landing spine are accepted; the operator-authored P1 proof-kernel node is active and fail-closed pending its first discriminating closing validation, while P2 and later product lanes remain parked. The toolchain pin model is an independent root. The P-derive receipt feeds the emitter fixed point, so the v1 chain nests under it. Compute owns the one contract everything else asks for work through — an exact subject in, a typed ending and the outputs it promised back out — and it sits ABOVE CI rather than inside it, because owning CI, converging the fleet, serving models and eventually judging changes are four consumers of one fabric, not four execution systems. It grounds on the signed realization spine rather than minting a second scheduler beside it. CI control owns who starts, queues and hands out required work and how its result reaches GitHub; CI cost owns how much computation that work performs. Fleet owns whether a merge to main becomes applied machine state and whether that question has one answer. Node fields define boundary, first slice, RED control, exclusions, owner, and handback."),
      ],
      concat(
        if focus_is_active(focus: focus) {
          [section_prose(content: focus_notice(
            focus: focus,
            nodes: nodes,
            edges: edges,
          ))]
        } else {
          []
        },
        [
          section_group(
            label: "",
            nodes: focus_visible_nodes(
              focus: focus,
              nodes: nodes,
              edges: edges,
            ),
            edges: focus_visible_edges(
              focus: focus,
              nodes: nodes,
              edges: edges,
            )
          ),
        ],
      ),
    ),
  }
}

fn accepted_node_ids_from_receipts(receipts: List<RoadmapAcceptanceReceipt>) -> List<RoadmapNodeId> {
  declared_roadmap_nodes()
    |> filter(n => node_accepted(receipts: receipts, rn: n))
    |> map(n => n.node)
}

fn roadmap_authority_projection_from_carrier_text(text: String) -> RoadmapAuthorityProjection {
  match roadmap_acceptance_receipts_from_carrier_text(text: text) {
    RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjected { receipts: receipts } =>
      RoadmapAuthorityProjected {
        doc: RoadmapDocument {
          preamble: preamble(),
          lead_lanes: [],
          sections: [
            active_section_for_acceptance(
              focus: roadmap_focus_selection,
              accepted: accepted_node_ids_from_receipts(receipts: receipts),
            ),
          ],
        },
        accepted: accepted_node_ids_from_receipts(receipts: receipts),
      }
    RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      RoadmapAuthorityProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
  }
}

fn roadmap_authority_projection() -> RoadmapAuthorityProjection {
  match accepted_roadmap_node_ids_projection() {
    AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      RoadmapAuthorityProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
    AcceptedRoadmapNodeIdsProjected { ids: accepted } =>
      RoadmapAuthorityProjected {
        doc: RoadmapDocument {
          preamble: preamble(),
          lead_lanes: [],
          sections: [active_section_for_acceptance(focus: roadmap_focus_selection, accepted: accepted)],
        },
        accepted: accepted,
      }
  }
}

fn active_section() -> RoadmapSection {
  active_section_for(focus: roadmap_focus_selection)
}

data roadmap_authority_focus_parameter_note: String = "roadmap_authority_for exists so a claim about the AUTHORITY can be written without a claim about the current VIEW. A witness that asserts which lanes are active, but reads the projected markdown to do it, silently becomes a witness about whatever focus happens to be set — it would go red the moment someone focused the page, having found nothing wrong with the roadmap at all. Passing NoFocus makes that independence explicit at the call site rather than leaving it to coincidence. roadmap_authority() keeps the declared focus, because the generated ROADMAP.md IS the focused view."

fn roadmap_authority_for(focus: RoadmapFocus) -> RoadmapDocument {
  RoadmapDocument {
    preamble: preamble(),
    lead_lanes: [],
    sections: [active_section_for(focus: focus)],
  }
}

fn active_section_for(focus: RoadmapFocus) -> RoadmapSection {
  match roadmap_authority_projection() {
    RoadmapAuthorityProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      RoadmapSection {
        title: "Active deliverables — dependency order",
        elements: [
          section_prose(content: concat("roadmap acceptance projection refused: ", detail)),
        ],
      }
    RoadmapAuthorityProjected { doc: _, accepted: accepted } =>
      active_section_for_acceptance(focus: focus, accepted: accepted)
  }
}

data guarantee_ladder_lane_note: String = "STATE vs WORK, and IN-GRAPH vs BESIDE-IT. Rung STATE has one authority — the guarantee claims carrier (ladder-claims-carrier node), its dispositions DERIVED from execution receipts and EMITTED by ladder-census-emitters — never a ticket; a climb node names its target transition and lets the census/carrier say where the class stands today (the 2026-07-31 operator verdict on gunbc#7489 caught headlines transcribing rungs, the third rung-inflation catch in this lane's own artifacts). The WORK rows below are ordinary declared roadmap nodes under lane owner compiler-guarantee: the first cut rendered them from a section-local list beside the graph, which minted a second roadmap universe — uncounted, receipt-unreachable, invisible to the identity/endpoint/acyclicity witnesses and to active_roadmap_nodes() (same verdict; review 45356 independently flagged the owner-less rows the placement forced). Owner here is the LANE's identity, not an assignee: nodes are unbound until dispatch binds a session and a closing contract, exactly like every other lane. Page visibility is the typed focus policy in roadmap_focus_selection (the lane is selected so the unstaffed backlog stays on the page), and rendered-node containment is witnessed — see roadmap_rendered_containment_note. Post-merge verdict (2026-07-31, on the merged gunbc#7489): v2-phase-carriers split into five staged nodes (its identity is the registry's first tombstone, superseded by v2-self-grounding-frontier); floor-parse-formation-wall, floor-record-construction-wall, and the compiler-accepted-obligation-closure terminal added; baseline anchored to the merge commit; and the standing discipline that an OPEN PR is candidate evidence, never main's rung — tickets cite open work as 'open candidate', and the census states main's disposition separately. Spine verdict (2026-08-01, seventh pass): the ladder-measurement-schema node now precedes probes and carrier (the receipt protocol both meet through); the three merged P0 slices are recut at their actual grain with tombstoned identities (floor-call-shape-wall, floor-method-existence-wall, floor-inhabitance-wall each split into an ACCEPTED fragment node plus open siblings); the Accepted door is split mechanism/floor/extended (compiler-accepted-obligation-closure tombstoned) so the audit form lands with the carrier; exemption removal re-grounded on the two grounding nodes; emitters parallel with the baseline; the baseline a two-revision execution. Correction on the split (review 45918): the first extended-activation cut kept requires-all edges on one node while its prose promised class-by-class widening — the graph would have deferred every admission behind the slowest climb; recut as four per-class admission nodes (each ready the day its own climb lands) with accepted-extended-obligation-closure the terminal roster-completeness certification, where requires-all honestly belongs. Sequencing: everything outside the spine's first increment (schema, then probes + carrier) and the authorized parallel containment workstream WAITS."

